Package: quadrf
Section: metapackages
Architecture: all
Depends: quadrf-boot (= ${binary:Version}),
         quadrf-common (= ${binary:Version}),
         quadrf-demos (= ${binary:Version}),
         quadrf-desktop (= ${binary:Version}),
         quadrf-fpga (= ${binary:Version}),
         quadrf-gui (= ${binary:Version}),
         quadrf-network (= ${binary:Version}),
         quadrf-soapy (= ${binary:Version}),
         quadrf-ups (= ${binary:Version}),
         ${misc:Depends}
Recommends: quadrf-phy, quadrf-meshtasticd
Description: software-defined radio appliance for the Raspberry Pi 5
 QuadRF turns a Raspberry Pi 5 and a Lattice ECP5 radio front-end into a
 self-contained SDR appliance: FPGA bring-up at boot, SoapySDR drivers, a web
 control panel, a remote desktop and the demo applications.
 .
 This metapackage pulls in the complete appliance. Install the individual
 quadrf-* packages instead to build a smaller system.
 .
 Also recommends quadrf-phy and quadrf-meshtasticd, the LoRa PHY and
 Meshtastic mesh daemon, so a plain apt-get install carries mesh support
 on by default; drop them with --no-install-recommends if you don't want
 it.

Package: quadrf-headless
Section: metapackages
Architecture: all
Depends: quadrf-boot (= ${binary:Version}),
         quadrf-common (= ${binary:Version}),
         quadrf-fpga (= ${binary:Version}),
         quadrf-gui (= ${binary:Version}),
         quadrf-network (= ${binary:Version}),
         quadrf-soapy (= ${binary:Version}),
         ${misc:Depends}
Recommends: quadrf-phy, quadrf-meshtasticd
Description: QuadRF appliance without the graphical desktop
 Radio, SoapySDR, networking and the web control panel, with no KasmVNC
 desktop, demo applications or GNU Radio. Suited to units that are only driven
 over the network.
 .
 Also recommends quadrf-phy and quadrf-meshtasticd (see quadrf's
 description); drop with --no-install-recommends if you don't want it.

Package: quadrf-common
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends}
Description: QuadRF shared configuration and control command
 Site configuration in /etc/quadrf/quadrf.conf, the shell helpers the other
 quadrf-* packages use in their maintainer scripts, and the quadrf command for
 checking state and re-applying configuration.

Package: quadrf-boot
Architecture: all
Depends: quadrf-common (= ${binary:Version}), ${misc:Depends}
Description: QuadRF firmware configuration and device-tree overlays
 Installs the fpga-csi and fpga-dsi overlays into the firmware partition and
 keeps the QuadRF block in config.txt and the USB gadget arguments in
 cmdline.txt up to date.
 .
 Changes here only take effect after a reboot; the package records that in
 /var/lib/quadrf/reboot-required.

Package: quadrf-fpga
Architecture: arm64
Depends: quadrf-boot (= ${binary:Version}),
         quadrf-common (= ${binary:Version}),
         quadrf-fpga-dkms (= ${source:Version}),
         quadrf-openocd | openocd,
         ${misc:Depends},
         ${shlibs:Depends}
Description: QuadRF FPGA bring-up and front-end control
 Bitstream, OpenOCD board configuration and the quadrf-jtag command that tunes
 the MAX285x front-end. load-quadrf.service programs the FPGA, loads the CSI
 and DSI drivers and initialises the radio at boot.

Package: quadrf-fpga-dkms
Architecture: all
Depends: dkms,
         linux-headers-rpi-2712 | linux-headers-rpi-v8 | raspberrypi-kernel-headers | linux-headers-arm64,
         ${misc:Depends}
Description: QuadRF CSI and DSI kernel drivers (DKMS)
 Source for the fpga-csi and fpga-dsi out-of-tree drivers. DKMS rebuilds them
 against each installed kernel, so a kernel upgrade does not need a manual
 rebuild as long as the matching headers are present.

Package: quadrf-soapy
Architecture: arm64
Depends: quadrf-common (= ${binary:Version}),
         quadrf-fpga (= ${binary:Version}),
         soapyremote-server,
         soapysdr-tools,
         ${misc:Depends},
         ${shlibs:Depends}
Description: SoapySDR drivers for the QuadRF front-end
 The mipi and quadrf SoapySDR modules, which expose the appliance to
 SoapySDRUtil, GNU Radio and any other SoapySDR client, plus a service that
 publishes the radio over SoapyRemote.

Package: quadrf-gui
Architecture: all
Depends: python3,
         python3-flask,
         python3-flask-socketio,
         quadrf-common (= ${binary:Version}),
         quadrf-fpga,
         ${misc:Depends}
Description: QuadRF web control panel
 Flask application on port 8080 with the receive and transmit controls, live
 status over WebSocket, demo launch/stop, Wi-Fi provisioning and hotspot
 setup. nginx publishes it at / when quadrf-network is installed.

Package: quadrf-network
Architecture: all
Depends: avahi-daemon,
         avahi-utils,
         dnsmasq,
         hostapd,
         isc-dhcp-server,
         iw,
         libnss-mdns,
         nginx,
         openssl,
         quadrf-common (= ${binary:Version}),
         sudo,
         wpasupplicant,
         ${misc:Depends}
Description: QuadRF networking, hotspot and web front end
 nginx in front of the control panel at /, the remote desktop at /VNC/ and
 the AR page; dnsmasq leases and names on the Ethernet, USB gadget and Wi-Fi
 interfaces; mDNS as HOSTNAME.local; and a fallback access point for when no
 known Wi-Fi network answers.

Package: quadrf-demos
Architecture: arm64
Depends: mpv,
         quadrf-common (= ${binary:Version}),
         quadrf-fpga (= ${binary:Version}),
         ${misc:Depends},
         ${shlibs:Depends}
Suggests: quadrf-soapy
Description: QuadRF demonstration applications
 Spatial RF Vision (swept-LO phase scatter with a WebSocket feed for the
 browser AR page), the PSD plot and the NTSC decoder, plus the phasegaze
 sources under /usr/share/quadrf/phasegaze. systemd units are started on
 demand from the control panel; they are not enabled at boot.

Package: quadrf-desktop
Architecture: all
Depends: dbus-x11,
         kasmvncserver,
         libglib2.0-bin,
         librsvg2-common,
         openbox,
         pcmanfm,
         qradiolink,
         quadrf-common (= ${binary:Version}),
         quadrf-demos,
         quadrf-gnuradio (= ${binary:Version}),
         tint2,
         wmctrl,
         x11-utils,
         x11-xserver-utils,
         xfce4-terminal,
         ${misc:Depends}
Recommends: lxde-icon-theme, xdg-utils
Description: QuadRF remote desktop session
 KasmVNC session on port 8444 running openbox with a tint2 panel and the
 QuadRF launchers, branded to match the appliance. Reached through nginx at
 /VNC/ or desktop.HOSTNAME.local.

Package: quadrf-gnuradio
Architecture: all
Depends: gir1.2-gtk-3.0,
         gnuradio,
         quadrf-common (= ${binary:Version}),
         ${misc:Depends}
Recommends: gr-osmosdr, quadrf-soapy
Description: GNU Radio flowgraphs for QuadRF
 Receive and transmit example flowgraphs for the QuadRF front-end, installed
 under /usr/share/quadrf/grc.

Package: quadrf-ups
Architecture: all
Depends: python3, python3-smbus, quadrf-common (= ${binary:Version}), ${misc:Depends}
Recommends: i2c-tools
Description: QuadRF UPS HAT monitor
 Reads the battery state over I2C and writes /tmp/ups_status for the desktop
 panel. Warns and shuts the appliance down when the cells run flat.
